kizinkiko joseph and his family have  been in this camp for displaced people  in bunia in ituri province since may  their village is at the buddha with  benny territory in neighboring north  kivu it's where one of the most feared  armed groups called allied democratic  forces or adf has been attacking  villages  adf have been fighting with government  forces the troops retreated then the  rebels started attacking us so we also  fled  the government declared a state of  emergency in may effectively placing  ituri and north kivu under military role  to counter the rebels but it has been a  struggle  since we've been here a convoy of  civilian vehicles as quoted by the army  was ambushed by adf several people were  killed and others are missing dozens of  vehicles were burned and the military  escorts have now been suspended a  village was also attacked and about 30  people killed  this camp was set up two years ago but  it is increasingly receiving more and  more people from areas that were not  previously caught up in the conflict it  is one of many idp camps carted across  this province hosting thousands of idps  who continue to flee from this conflict  the new presence of adf in some parts of  ituri further complicates an  ethnic-fueled conflict that's gone on  for decades mainly between lando farmers  and hammer harders more than 1 000  people mostly hammers have been killed  since 2017 and close to 2 million people  displaced the total needs for the drc  were estimated at 1.98  billion dollars which is nearly 2  billion  to aid  16 million people as the most vulnerable  the most vulnerable areas of the country  are ituri north kivu south kivu and the  north of northern tanganyika these areas  where there's so much displacement  require special attention  and unfortunately  at the moment this year we are only 30  percent funded of our humanitarian  response plan  government forces are pushing rebels out  of some areas and people are beginning  to return in this village of nyakunde  those who've come back are receiving  essential supplies to restart their  lives  melchion zanamusi and his wife chantal  say they fled during fighting between  troops and a militia group  we did not find anything in our homes  when we returned  we were starting our lives over  everything was looted  military victories like this one in  yakune are boosting the morale of  soldiers as well as the confidence of  those they are here to protect but some  here say the successes are too few and  quite far apart  catherine soy al-jazeera ituri province
 

